bin/
obj/
dist/
Installer/obj/
Installer/Payload/
*.user
*.suo
.vs/

# The output of the last bench run: it changes at every launch and is not a
# measurement until it has a format. That is decided by entry F2; until
# then it stays a working file.
Bench/ultimo-giro.txt
Bench/ultimo-ponte.txt

# The journal of the run IN PROGRESS, line by line with the time next to it. It is the file that
# is looked at while the bench runs, to know how far it has got and whether it is stuck:
# it lasts as long as the run and the next one rewrites it.
Bench/in-corso.txt

# The report of every run (entry F2): one .md and one .json per launch, plus
# `last.json`. They change at every run and are the measurement of THIS machine at
# THIS moment: versioning them would fill the history with files nobody
# rereads and that diverge at every merge. The number that counts — the one that goes in
# a README or a decision — is copied by hand, with the date.
# THE CONTENT IS EXCLUDED, NOT THE FOLDER: with `Bench/report/` git does not
# even enter it, and the exception line below would have no effect.
# It is a defect that gives no error — the file is simply not versioned,
# and the CI says «the bench was not run» forever.
Bench/report/*
# ... EXCEPT `last.json`, and it is not an exception of convenience (entry F9).
# It is the only proof that someone ran the bench: the CI cannot
# drive SOLIDWORKS, but it can REFUSE A DELIVERY in which nobody did.
# Without this file in the repository that check does not exist.
!Bench/report/last.json

# One line per run, at the end: it serves to COUNT the checks that fail at random
# (entry F12). It is the log of THIS machine — whoever runs the bench elsewhere
# has their own runs, and mixing them in a versioned file would give a count that
# belongs to nobody. The number, when it is needed to decide, is copied into the TODO with
# the date.
Bench/storico.txt

# The parts the bench creates while testing: disposable, and with machine
# paths inside. The cases name them with `{BANCO}`, which the runner resolves
# here — so that no case carries around the name of whoever works.
Bench/lavoro/

# The material of measurement V2: twenty PNGs and five states, ~1.5 MB, redone by
# `collect_views.js` in a minute. It is not the measurement — the measurement is the number
# that ends up in the README — and a repository does not carry around the frames
# from which it was derived. The PLANS on the other hand are versioned (`cases/views/`):
# those are the part that must be repeatable.
Bench/viste/

# The plans that an isolated model writes in measurement V3: it redoes them at every
# run, and versioning them would mean freezing the answer of ONE model of ONE
# day as if it were the measurement. The measurement is the number, and it is in the README.
Bench/cases/forma/

# `bin/` and `obj/` above are enough for Bench/ too, and it is intended that the
# Dassault libraries do not even end up there: the bench csproj
# references them with Private=False, so they are not copied into bin/.
# Two defenses for the same trouble, because redistributing a Dassault binary
# is the kind of mistake that is discovered on the day of publication.

# The cleanup patterns specific to a plant: they name internal things
# (server names, network folders), and a versioned tool that names them
# publishes exactly what it was meant to remove. The mechanism is in
# Checks/cleanup-history.js and is public; the names stay on each machine.
#
# THE RULE IS WIDE ON PURPOSE, and before it was the name of that file only. The
# `.local.js` convention had already been born and worked: widening it now excludes
# the NEXT file with plant names inside before it exists, instead of the
# day after it has been published.
Checks/*.local.js

# The notes that are only for us. They are:
#
#   TODO.md                 the work journal: seventeen thousand lines that
#                           name the author, the workstations, the decisions
#                           still to be made and the judgments on other people's projects.
#                           Five probes check it and keep doing so from
#                           here; on a clone that does not have it they stop saying
#                           that they did not check (Checks/where-is-the-todo.js).
#   CONFRONTO.md            the comparison with someone else's project, by name.
#   PROVE-INSTALLATORE.md   the results of ONE acceptance session of
#                           14/9/2026, stopped at Installer.exe 0.7.1.
#
# WITHOUT THIS LINE THE FIRST `git add -A` PUTS THEM BACK IN. And it is exactly
# how they ended up there the first time: nobody had ever excluded them.
note-interne/

# The backups that the installer makes of the client configuration before
# touching it: they have the timestamp in the name and are born in the folder where
# testing happens. Today none is tracked; it costs one line to keep them out
# before one is born during a test and ends up in a commit.
*.bak

# The agents' worktrees (workflow): separate working copies, never versioned.
.claude/
